<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Ancón</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1883">1883</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-pacific">War of the Pacific</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Ancón, signed on 20 October 1883, ended the war between Chile and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-pacific">War of the Pacific</a>. Peru ceded the nitrate province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarapaca">Tarapacá</a> to Chile, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacna">Tacna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arica">Arica</a> were to remain under Chilean control for ten years, followed by a plebiscite. The plebiscite was never held, and the dispute was settled only by the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> of 1929.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ancon.jpg" alt="Treaty of Ancón" width="320" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Janitoalevic · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Treaty_of_Anc%C3%B3n.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-pacific">War of the Pacific</a> and the Chilean occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> several rival Peruvian governments arose. The Chilean administration set up a collaborationist government at La Magdalena under Francisco García Calderón to represent the occupied country in negotiations, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolas-de-pierola">Nicolás de Piérola</a>'s constitutional government moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayacucho">Ayacucho</a> and a third, under Lizardo Montero, sat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arequipa">Arequipa</a>. In 1883 Chile recognised a fourth government, under Miguel Iglesias, who wanted peace at any cost, including the loss of territory. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andres-avelino-caceres">Andrés Avelino Cáceres</a>, who opposed him, was defeated at the Battle of Huamachuco on 10 July 1883. The treaty was signed at Ancón, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Article 2 ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarapaca">Tarapacá</a> to Chile unconditionally, and Article 13 required recognition of the Chilean administrative and judicial acts of the occupation. Article 3, the most controversial, left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacna">Tacna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arica">Arica</a> under Chilean administration for ten years, after which a plebiscite would decide their fate and the country chosen would pay $10,000,000 to the other. Chilean historians hold that it was a covert transfer of both provinces, which Peruvian historians such as Jorge Basadre dispute.</p><p>Articles 4 to 10 dealt with the guano and saltpetre deposits: half the profits from guano in deposits already known were to go to paying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>'s debt, and the Lobos de Afuera Islands were to stay under Chilean exploitation while their guano lasted, with half the profits for Peru. Article 12 set up tribunals to compensate Chilean citizens expelled from Peru at the start of the war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Discontent with the treaty led to the Peruvian Civil War of 1884–1885, which overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iglesias">Iglesias</a>. The Castellón-Elías protocol of 8 January 1890 gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> practically all the guano exploitation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarapaca">Tarapacá</a> on condition that it pass it to the creditors. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacna">Tacna</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arica">Arica</a> question was settled by the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> in 1929, through the mediation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> under President Herbert Hoover: Arica went to Chile, while Peru regained Tacna and received USD 6 million. A clause barring Chile from ceding former Peruvian territory to another nation without asking Peru was invoked in 1975, when Peru rejected a Chilean offer of minor ports to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Ancón” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
